questions.solution_text:
The limitations have been explicitly presented in the final paragraph: {...Not only were the batteries deployed only in mice, but they also were used against a mouse-specific breast cancer... of course, in humans. Xia, Fan Zhang and their colleagues are already thinking about how the battery might have to be made more flexible and more powerful to work on human-sized tumors — using battery energy to starve cancer of air...}
Based on the above, Option C is the most accurate because it correctly identifies that the study was conducted only on mice, and further testing is needed to ensure safety and efficacy in humans. (This is an important limitation to consider, as animal studies do not always translate directly to human treatments). The researchers also note that more research would be needed to ensure that the battery could be made flexible and powerful enough to work on human-sized tumors, and that it would be safe and effective for use in humans. While Options A, B and D could be valid points, they have not been discussed or implied in the passage.
Hence, Option C is the correct choice.

{The great majority of the people we would consider reasonable and accountable (myself included) will dismiss these claims by laughing at the very absurdity of the hypothesis}
The author's stance on the moon landings is that they happened and that the evidence supporting their occurrence is stronger than the counter-evidence put forth by conspiracy theorists. The author presents the case of the Flat Earth Society's claims that the moon landings were staged, but then goes on to state that most people would dismiss these claims as absurd. The author also acknowledges that he himself has not personally investigated the evidence for or against the moon landings; however, the author does not suggest that this lack of personal investigation makes it difficult to determine whether or not the moon landings occurred [Option C]. Rather, the author seems to be arguing that the weight of the evidence is such that most reasonable people would accept the reality of the moon landings. Option A, therefore, correctly reflects the author's position on the subject. Since the author does offer his opinion on the moon landings, we can eliminate Options B and D. 
Hence, Option A is the correct choice.

questions.passage_text:
Read the following and answer the questions.

The US swimming team consists of seven people Hall, Nathan, Spitz, Phelps, Jagger, Schooling and Lochte. Of the seven swimmers, it is known that 

a) One of the seven swimmers does not use the swimming pool on Tuesdays, Thursdays and Saturdays. 

b) Another one does not use the pool on Tuesdays and Sundays; 

c) Another one does not use the pool on Mondays, Tuesdays and Wednesdays; 

d) Another one does not use on Fridays and Saturdays; 

e) Another one does not use on Mondays, Fridays and Saturdays; 

f) Another one does not use on Thursdays; 

g) Another one does not use on Saturdays and Sundays.

All of them use the pool on all other days 

Further, it is known that in any week,

i. The number of days on which both Jagger and Spitz use the pool is atleast equal to 4.
ii. The number of days on which both Spitz and Schooling use the pool is exactly equal to 4.
iii. Phelps swims on all days that Lochte swims.
iv. The number of days on which both Hall and Jagger don't use the pool is exactly equal to two.

